  "answer_text": "**Medi-Cal** is California\u2019s Medicaid program, which provides free or low-cost health coverage for eligible California residents, including low-income individuals, families, seniors, pregnant women, and people with disabilities. \n\n---\n\n### 1. General Eligibility Requirements\nFor most adults, eligibility is based primarily on your **income**:\n* **Adults (Ages 19\u201364):** Income must generally be at or below **138% of the Federal Poverty Level (FPL)**. For a single person, this is roughly $21,600/year.\n* **Children (Ages 0\u201319):** Families can qualify with incomes up to **266% of the FPL**.\n* **Pregnant Individuals:** Eligibility limits are typically higher to ensure coverage during and after pregnancy.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Major Changes (2026 Rules & Reinstatements)\nDue to recent California budget legislation, several significant changes took effect. If you are applying for or renewing your Medi-Cal, take note of these new rules:\n\n* **Asset Limits Reinstated (Effective January 1, 2026):** \n  * *Who it affects:* Older adults (65+), people with disabilities, and individuals needing long-term nursing home care (non-MAGI Medi-Cal). (Asset tests still do not apply to standard, income-based MAGI Medi-Cal for younger adults and families).\n  * *The limit:* Countable assets are now limited to **$130,000 for an individual**, plus $65,000 for each additional family member (up to 10 people). \n  * *What is exempt:* Your primary home, one vehicle, personal belongings, and some retirement accounts are generally not counted toward this limit.\n* **Immigration Status & Enrollment Freeze (Effective January 1, 2026):**\n  * *Who it affects:* Undocumented adults (ages 19 and older) who do not have a Satisfactory Immigration Status (SIS).\n  * *The freeze:* While California previously expanded full-scope Medi-Cal to all residents, a **new enrollment freeze** prevents undocumented adults from signing up for *new* full-scope coverage. \n  * *Existing members:* If you were enrolled in full-scope Medi-Cal before January 1, 2026, you will keep your full coverage as long as you complete your renewals on time.\n  * *Exceptions:* Undocumented children under 19 and pregnant individuals remain eligible for full-scope Medi-Cal.\n* **Dental Coverage Changes (Effective July 1, 2026):**\n  * Undocumented adults (19+) will lose full dental benefits and will transition to **emergency dental coverage only**. Full dental benefits will remain available only for children (under 19) and pregnant individuals.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What Does Medi-Cal Cover?\nMedi-Cal covers \"essential health benefits\", including:\n* **Outpatient services:** Doctor visits and checkups.\n* **Emergency care:** Emergency room visits and urgent care.\n* **Hospitalization:** Inpatient care and surgeries.\n* **Prescription drugs:** Medi-Cal Rx covers approved medications.\n* **Maternity and newborn care**.\n* **Mental health & substance use treatment**.\n* **Preventive services:** Immunizations, screenings, and chronic disease management.\n* **Pediatric services:** Including vision and dental for children.\n\n---\n\n### 4. How to Apply\nYou can apply for Medi-Cal at any time of year using one of the following methods:\n* **Online:** \n  * **BenefitsCal:** [BenefitsCal.com](https://benefitscal.com) (Best if applying for other benefits like CalFresh/food stamps).\n  * **Covered California:** [CoveredCA.com](https://www.coveredca.com) (If you do not qualify for Medi-Cal, you will automatically be screened for discounted private insurance).\n* **By Phone:** Call your local county social services office or the Covered California service center.\n* **By Mail / In Person:** Download and print an application to mail in, or visit your county's Department of Social Services office to apply with a caseworker in person.\n\n### 5. How to Keep Your Medi-Cal (Renewals)\nIf you already have Medi-Cal, the state is actively sending out yellow envelope renewal packets. **Do not ignore these packets.** You must complete and return them, or log into your online account to complete your renewal, to prevent your coverage from being paused or canceled.",
